The fog had been clinging to the cliffs of Blackwood Point for three days, a thick, gray shroud that muffled the world and turned the lighthouse into a solitary eye staring into the void. Elias, the keeper, wiped the condensation from the lantern room’s glass, his breath pluming in the cold air. He was a man of routine, of winding the clockwork and polishing the brass, but today, the routine felt heavy.

As he descended the spiral stairs to the base of the tower, his boot caught on something hard. He looked down. Half-buried in the wet sand, just below the tide line, was a bottle. It wasn’t the usual glass green of the sea; it was a deep, murky amber, and it was sealed with wax that had long since cracked.

Elias picked it up. It was heavy. He shook it, and a faint *clink* echoed from within.

"Curious," he muttered, his voice sounding thin in the vast, echoing chamber.

He carried it up to his small, cluttered office, placing it on the desk beside a cold cup of tea. With a pocketknife, he carefully sliced the wax seal. The cork popped out with a sigh,
